faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour. People leave a workshop enthusiastic and are back to their old habits within a fortnight, because nobody built a new habit on real work in one day. Our program runs across three months on your practice's own files, so by the end it is simply how your team works — and ongoing support keeps it current after that, which a workshop by definition cannot."
  - q: "Will this work for staff who aren't technical?"
    a: "That's exactly who it's built for. No coding, no setup, no prompt-engineering theory. If someone can write an email to a client, they can do this — the program starts from that assumption rather than treating it as a limitation."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement — you know the total before you start. Tell us how many people you'd send and we'll give you the number on the call, along with the tasks we'd expect to compress first so you can judge it against the hours."
  - q: "Does AI do the accounting itself?"
    a: "It does the writing and reading around the work, which is most of the week. The judgement, the review and the sign-off stay with the accountant, which is what your professional obligations require and what we design the workflows around. That boundary is deliberate, and it's why the training holds up under a QA review."
  - q: "We're a five-person practice. Is this only for big firms?"
    a: "Small practices usually get more out of it, because there's nobody to delegate the drafting to. The program is priced and paced for SME teams, and it's the same three months whether you send two people or twenty."
  - q: "When should we start, given tax season?"
    a: "Before it. Practices that come through in autumn go into the crunch with the follow-up and drafting workflows already built, which is when the saving is worth the most. If you're reading this mid-season, the audit takes a minute and the conversation can wait until you surface."
  - q: "Do we need to buy new software?"
    a: "Not to start. Most practices already have a paid ChatGPT, Copilot or Claude seat somewhere in the business, and the training is tool-agnostic in any case — your team learns the thinking, then applies it in whatever your practice standardises on."
